\section{Separate Three Kinds of Rewrite}

In ``A Note on Tree Rewriting vs. Tree Covering,'' Blindell contrasts choosing a compatible set of patterns on a tree with repeatedly replacing matched parts of it until a goal remains \cite[section~3.5]{blindell2016instruction}. For ordinary instruction patterns these can describe the same derivation: reducing the chosen tiles in operand order presents their goal symbols to each enclosing tile. There is no need to mutate the IR just to carry out such a derivation.

Two other operations can look similar on paper. A \emph{chain} changes the form promised at the same node, without matching another IR operator. It may emit an instruction, for example to transfer to another register bank. A \emph{canonicalization} changes the subject tree before matching, preserving the computation but possibly exposing or hiding particular patterns. Blindell's discussion of BURS grammars with terminals on both sides of a production is a reminder that tree transformations can also be included in the search itself \cite[section~3.6.3]{blindell2016instruction}. The separate canonicalization below is a simpler engineering choice, not a claim that every such search can throw away all but its canonical form.

\section{Orient the Shape Changes}

Take pure, nontrapping, fixed-width word expressions with no hidden flag effects. For bitwise $\mathrm{xor}$ use two directed rewrites: move a literal on the left to the right when the right operand is nonliteral, and erase an exclusive-or with literal zero on the right. The first uses commutativity, and the second uses the identity element. Do not install the inverse rules. A postorder normalizer applies these rewrites at the current node until neither applies, after first normalizing its children.

Termination follows from the lexicographic pair consisting of total node count and the number of literal-left, nonliteral-right exclusive-ors. Erasing an identity strictly reduces node count. A swap preserves node count and removes one misoriented node without changing the literal/nonliteral classification of its root for its parent. When erasure exposes a subtree, that subtree has already been normalized. An enclosing exclusive-or might newly see a literal, but it will be inspected later in postorder. The resulting normal form is relative to these rules; for example it neither reassociates exclusive-ors nor recognizes every expression equal to zero.

\section{Charge Transfers as Part of the Cover}

Let $G$ hold a word. For the Boolean result of $\mathrm{iszero}$, let $F$ denote a condition flag, $P$ a predicate register, and $M$ a word mask (all ones for true, all zeros for false). These are representations of the same abstract truth value. An incoming argument supplies $G$ for free. In the grammar below $\mathrm{lit}_5$ is a literal whose unsigned value fits in five bits, while $\mathrm{lit}$ accepts any word literal. The direct mask instruction is an alternative to transferring a flag through the predicate bank.

\[
\begin{array}{lr@{\qquad}lr}
\text{Production} & \text{Cost} & \text{Production} & \text{Cost}\\
G\gets\mathrm{lit} & 2 & F\gets\mathrm{iszero}(G) & 1\\
G\gets\mathrm{xor}(G,G) & 2 & M\gets\mathrm{iszero}(G) & 5\\
G\gets\mathrm{xor}(G,\mathrm{lit}_5) & 1 & P\gets F & 2\\
 & & M\gets P & 1
\end{array}
\]

Blindell's ``Handling Chain Rules in Purely Coverage-Driven Designs'' compares closure, inserting auxiliary nodes, and injecting conversions after selection \cite[section~3.5.1]{blindell2016instruction}. In this case the chain graph is acyclic, so closure needs only one pass in dependency order. Late injection cannot generally price the choice between a conversion path and a directly produced mask.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Normalize, Label, and Recover a Mask}

\begin{verbatim}
subject: iszero(xor(0, xor(31, arg(a))))

[1] normalize inner xor: xor(arg(a), 31)
    swap outer xor, then erase its right zero
    subject becomes iszero(xor(arg(a), 31))

    xor at G: min(generic 2 + 0 + 2, immediate 1 + 0) = 1
    iszero: seed F = 1 + 1 = 2; seed M = 5 + 1 = 6
[2] close iszero: P = F + 2 = 4; M = min(6, P + 1) = 5

    recover M via P via F at the iszero node
    recover G at xor; emit XOR-immediate, test, and transfers
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{[1]} Neither of these rewrites emits machine code. The normalized subject exposes the immediate on the encodable side, and $31$ is the five-bit maximum. Replacing it by $32$ leaves the normalized shape intact but requires the generic exclusive-or at cost four. All three finite results at the zero test now cost three more, so the mask costs eight instead of five.

\noindent\textbf{[2]} Both chain steps operate at the same IR node. The costs include actual flag-to-predicate and predicate-to-mask actions. If the latter costs three instead of one, the chain path costs seven and the direct mask rule wins at six; appending transfers after choosing the flag would miss that result.

\Cref{fig:visual-269} treats the two ways to obtain \(M\) as paths through the same-node goal graph. The common XOR cost is included in the input to each path.

